DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       This  function displays a message described by its arguments on the de‐
       vice(s) specified in the _c_l_a_s_s_i_f_i_c_a_t_i_o_n argument.  For messages written
       to _s_t_d_e_r_r, the format depends on the MMSSGGVVEERRBB environment variable.

       The _l_a_b_e_l argument identifies the source of the  message.   The  string
       must  consist of two colon separated parts where the first part has not
       more than 10 and the second part not more than 14 characters.

       The _t_e_x_t argument describes the condition of the error.

       The _a_c_t_i_o_n argument describes possible steps to recover from the error.
       If it is printed, it is prefixed by "TO FIX: ".

       The _t_a_g argument is a reference to the online documentation where  more
       information  can  be  found.   It  should contain the _l_a_b_e_l value and a
       unique identification number.

   DDuummmmyy aarrgguummeennttss
       Each of the arguments can have a dummy value.  The dummy classification
       value MMMM__NNUULLLLMMCC (0L)  does  not  specify  any  output,  so  nothing  is
       printed.   The dummy severity value NNOO__SSEEVV (0) says that no severity is
       supplied.  The values MMMM__NNUULLLLLLBBLL,  MMMM__NNUULLLLTTXXTT,  MMMM__NNUULLLLAACCTT,  MMMM__NNUULLLLTTAAGG
       are  synonyms  for  _(_(_c_h_a_r _*_) _0_), the empty string, and MMMM__NNUULLLLSSEEVV is a
       synonym for NNOO__SSEEVV.

   TThhee ccllaassssiiffiiccaattiioonn aarrgguummeenntt
       The _c_l_a_s_s_i_f_i_c_a_t_i_o_n argument is the sum of values describing 4 types  of
       information.

       The first value defines the output channel.

       MMMM__PPRRIINNTT    Output to _s_t_d_e_r_r.

       MMMM__CCOONNSSOOLLEE  Output to the system console.

       MMMM__PPRRIINNTT || MMMM__CCOONNSSOOLLEE
                   Output to both.

       The second value is the source of the error:

       MMMM__HHAARRDD     A hardware error occurred.

       MMMM__FFIIRRMM     A firmware error occurred.

       MMMM__SSOOFFTT     A software error occurred.

       The third value encodes the detector of the problem:

       MMMM__AAPPPPLL     It is detected by an application.

       MMMM__UUTTIILL     It is detected by a utility.

       MMMM__OOPPSSYYSS    It is detected by the operating system.

       The fourth value shows the severity of the incident:

       MMMM__RREECCOOVVEERR  It is a recoverable error.

       MMMM__NNRREECCOOVV   It is a nonrecoverable error.

   TThhee sseevveerriittyy aarrgguummeenntt
       The _s_e_v_e_r_i_t_y argument can take one of the following values:

       MMMM__NNOOSSEEVV    No severity is printed.

       MMMM__HHAALLTT     This value is printed as HALT.

       MMMM__EERRRROORR    This value is printed as ERROR.

       MMMM__WWAARRNNIINNGG  This value is printed as WARNING.

       MMMM__IINNFFOO     This value is printed as INFO.

       The  numeric  values  are between 0 and 4.  Using aaddddsseevveerriittyy(3) or the
       environment variable SSEEVV__LLEEVVEELL you can add more levels and  strings  to
       print.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEE
       The function can return 4 values:

       MMMM__OOKK       Everything went smooth.

       MMMM__NNOOTTOOKK    Complete failure.

       MMMM__NNOOMMSSGG    Error writing to _s_t_d_e_r_r.

       MMMM__NNOOCCOONN    Error writing to the console.

EENNVVIIRROONNMMEENNTT
       The  environment  variable MMSSGGVVEERRBB ("message verbosity") can be used to
       suppress parts of the output to _s_t_d_e_r_r.  (It does not influence  output
       to  the console.)  When this variable is defined, is non‐NULL, and is a
       colon‐separated list of valid keywords, then only the parts of the mes‐
       sage corresponding to these keywords is printed.   Valid  keywords  are
       "label", "severity", "text", "action", and "tag".

       The  environment variable SSEEVV__LLEEVVEELL can be used to introduce new sever‐
       ity levels.  By default, only the five severity levels described  above
       are available.  Any other numeric value would make ffmmttmmssgg() print noth‐
       ing.  If the user puts SSEEVV__LLEEVVEELL with a format like

              SEV_LEVEL=[description[:description[:...]]]

       in  the  environment  of the process before the first call to ffmmttmmssgg(),
       where each description is of the form

              severity‐keyword,level,printstring

       then ffmmttmmssgg() will also accept the indicated values for the  level  (in
       addition to the standard levels 0–4), and use the indicated printstring
       when such a level occurs.

       The  severity‐keyword  part  is  not  used by ffmmttmmssgg() but it has to be
       present.  The level part is a string representation of a  number.   The
       numeric value must be a number greater than 4.  This value must be used
       in  the  severity argument of ffmmttmmssgg() to select this class.  It is not
       possible to overwrite any of the predefined classes.   The  printstring
       is  the  string  printed  when  a message of this class is processed by
       ffmmttmmssgg().

AATTTTRRIIBBUUTTEESS
       For an explanation of the terms  used  in  this  section,  see  aattttrriibb‐‐
       uutteess(7).  allbox; lb lb lbx l l l.  Interface Attribute Value T{
       ffmmttmmssgg() T}   Thread safety  T{ glibc >= 2.16: MT‐Safe; glibc < 2.16:
       MT‐Unsafe T}

       Before glibc 2.16, the ffmmttmmssgg() function uses a static variable that is
       not protected, so it is not thread‐safe.

       Since glibc 2.16, the ffmmttmmssgg() function uses a lock to protect the
       static variable, so it is thread‐safe.

